          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Marcus Aurelius</strong><br>\nMarcus Aurelius was born on April 26, 121 AD, in Rome, into a prominent family. Raised in a period known as the Pax Romana or \"Roman Peace,\" he was educated by some of the finest tutors in Latin, Greek, rhetoric, and philosophy. As a young man, he was adopted by Emperor Antoninus Pius, which placed him in the line of succession for the Roman throne.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nAfter being adopted by Antoninus Pius in 138 AD, Marcus became his heir. He took the throne in 161 AD after Antoninus' death. Unlike other emperors, Marcus Aurelius held the title \"philosopher king\" because of his unique approach to leadership, blending stoic philosophy with the practical duties of ruling an empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Co-Emperorship</strong><br>\nMarcus Aurelius broke with tradition by elevating Lucius Verus to the role of co-emperor. This was the first time Rome was consistently ruled by two emperors. They ruled together until Verus' death in 169 AD. The partnership was marked by mutual respect and shared responsibilities across the vast Roman Empire.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
