          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Stoicism and Philosophy</strong><br>\nAs a Stoic philosopher, Marcus Aurelius wrote \"Meditations,\" a series of personal writings that recorded his private notes to himself and ideas on Stoic philosophy. These writings reveal his innermost thoughts on virtue, justice, and duty, offering insights into his mind and the philosophical underpinnings of his rule.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nMarcus Aurelius is known for his military campaigns in the Marcomannic Wars, fought against Germanic tribes. His reign was marked by several military challenges, which he managed personally despite his philosophical inclination for peace. He spent much of his time on campaign in the Danubian provinces.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Antonine Plague</strong><br>\nUnder Marcus Aurelius, the Roman Empire faced the devastating Antonine Plague, which killed millions. The plague significantly weakened the empire, causing a labor shortage and military recruitment difficulties. Marcus' leadership during this crisis was tested as he struggled to keep the empire stable.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
