          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Legal and Administrative Reforms</strong><br>\nMarcus Aurelius is credited with implementing a series of legal and administrative reforms. He aimed to make laws more humane, with a particular concern for the treatment of slaves and the vulnerable. His governance reflected his philosophical beliefs in fairness and the common good.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Succession</strong><br>\nDespite his stoic view of life, Marcus Aurelius faced personal tragedy, including the death of several children. His marriage to Faustina the Younger resulted in a large family, but only one son, Commodus, would survive to succeed him. Commodus' reign later marked a departure from his father's stoic principles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nMarcus Aurelius died in 180 AD, in Vindobona (modern-day Vienna) or Sirmium in Pannonia. His death marked the end of the Pax Romana, as the period of relative peace and stability began to deteriorate under his successors. Today, he is remembered as a symbol of virtuous leadership and is often cited as the last of the \"Five Good Emperors\" of Rome.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
